{
  "schemaVersion": "repair-regate/1",
  "readMeFirst": "data/r2-regate.json records the repair loop re-measured with compiler/eval/spike's gate in front of it, and names the two LTO probes as the thing it does NOT cover: they are gated the same way, they write no tracked rows, and so there was nothing in data/ of theirs for a digest comparison to be about. This file closes that by a different route. The probes still write no tracked rows, so what is compared is the cell TEXTS tools/LTO.md quotes against the readings a gated run produces. The entries below were derived from the run logs and not from LTO.md -- generating them from LTO.md would make the comparison vacuous by construction -- and test/lto-regate.test.mjs reads LTO.md's tables back out and compares every claimed cell, for every run, including denominators and the HELD word.",
  "question": "do the readings tools/LTO.md quotes for the two LTO probes reproduce with the spike/recovery gate in front of the run, at every level and both selection idioms?",
  "measuredOn": "2026-09-13",
  "whyItWasOpen": "The gate was wired into both probes on 2026-09-12 (lto-probe.mjs:278, lto-probe-gcc.mjs:312). LTO.md's last content change was earlier the same day, so every number in it was taken before the gate existed -- the code was gated and the prose was not, the same split r2-regate.json records for the rows. LTO.md did not contain the word gate.",
  "firstAttemptWasNarrowerAndSaidSoWrongly": "The first version of this record covered -O2 only and justified that with a technical claim: that -O2 is the one level at which the gate has a discriminating pair registered per vendor. That was FALSE. The gate was run at -O1, -O3 and -Os on both vendors and came back ESTABLISHED with injection RED and 1/1 discriminating each time; only -O0 is non-discriminating, which is all the probes' own comments ever said. The restriction had been cost dressed as capability, so the repair was to run the levels rather than to reword the excuse. It also read gate.configurations as 2/2 off the summary line; that is the per-configuration recovery count printed beside it, and verdict.configurations is 1/1 for a one-vendor one-level call.",
  "toolchain": {
    "cc": "Ubuntu clang version 18.1.3 (1ubuntu1)",
    "lld": "Ubuntu LLD 18.1.3 (compatible with GNU linkers)",
    "gcc": "gcc-13 (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0",
    "ld": "GNU ld (GNU Binutils for Ubuntu) 2.42, through the driver and gcc's LTO linker plugin",
    "node": "v18.19.1"
  },
  "coverage": {
    "levels": "-O1, -O2, -O3 and -Os on both vendors",
    "idioms": "the tracked eliminated files, and every removable-idiom file (--all-removable), on both vendors",
    "forms": "clang: full and thin LTO, separately. gcc: the one form gcc has",
    "secondBinaryAt": "-O2 on each vendor was additionally run with a second build of the same source, configured differently (Release vs the cmake default) and several times the size. Reproducing a reading with the gate added AND the binary changed separates neither, so the run the claim rests on is the one made with the exact binary LTO.md quotes; the other is evidence of something else -- that the readings do not depend on the build configuration.",
    "whyNotMinusO0": "At -O0 the gate is NOT established: discriminating reads 0/1, because the two spikes are registered to read the same word there. Both probes refuse such a run (exit 3) rather than passing it, so there is no -O0 LTO reading to take and LTO.md quotes none.",
    "rows": "18 entries over 15 DISTINCT LTO.md rows: the three -O2 rows reproduced twice (clang full/thin and gcc A) appear once per binary, so 18 is the number of (row, run) pairs and 15 the number of rows. An earlier wording said \"18 rows\", which counted the pairs."
  },
